# Task: in-progress-phase-columns

## Original input

> надо внутри колонки in progress создать несколько клолнок в котором таски будут раскидываться по фазам рисерч/дизайн/планирование

## Clarifications

- **How is the phase determined?** Derived from the task's attached files written by the delivery workflow (`task`, `research`, `design`, `plan`). No new frontmatter field, no tool/schema changes; purely read-side.
- **Which sub-columns?** Four: research, design, planning, implementation.
- **Configurable?** No. The phase set and file mapping are hardcoded to match the existing workflow skills.

Derived mapping (latest artifact present wins):

| Attached files present | Phase sub-column |
|---|---|
| `plan` | implementation |
| `design` (no `plan`) | planning |
| `research` (no `design`) | design |
| only `task`, or no workflow files | research |

Assumption: a task with no workflow files at all lands in `research` (the earliest phase), so every in-progress task appears in exactly one sub-column.

Clarifications gathered after research (answers from the user):

- **Legacy `.md` names count.** `research.md`, `design.md`, `plan.md` (and `task.md`) map to the same phases as the suffix-less names.
- **Matching is case-insensitive.** `Plan`, `PLAN.md` etc. count.
- **A task sits in the column of its furthest phase.** Where a card groups several in-progress tasks (a parent with nested in-progress subtasks), the card goes to the furthest phase reached by any task in that group (interpretation of "таск должен находиться в колонке максимально последней фазы").
- **Sub-columns are nested inside the In Progress column**, not promoted to top-level board columns. To do and Done stay as they are.
- **Counters: both.** The In Progress header keeps its total count, and each phase sub-column shows its own count.

Clarification after the first design (user's answer to "four sub-columns don't fit side by side"):

- **The phase sub-columns are virtual, overlapping lanes.** They are not laid out side by side; each next phase is shifted right by half a card width relative to the previous one (Research at offset 0, Design at ½ card, Planning at 1 card, Implementation at 1½ cards). Cards stay stacked vertically inside the In Progress column, so lanes overlap horizontally while cards never collide. This replaces the 2×2 / stacked layout from the first design.

## Problem statement

The kanban board's In Progress column lumps all in-progress tasks together, so a human cannot see which workflow phase each task is in; the column must be split into research / design / planning / implementation sub-columns, with each task placed according to the workflow artifacts attached to it.

## Acceptance criteria

- The In Progress column on the board renders four sub-columns in order: Research, Design, Planning, Implementation.
- Each in-progress task appears in exactly one sub-column, chosen by the mapping above.
- Todo and Done columns are unchanged.
- Phase derivation is exposed through `task.Service` (e.g. the board snapshot in `internal/task/view.go`); `internal/web` does not touch storage directly.
- The web UI stays read-only: only GET routes, no mutating service calls, handlers still use `Resolver.Current()`.
- Existing card features (danger-zone highlighting, branch chip, detail link, htmx polling refresh) keep working inside the sub-columns.
- Layout remains usable at narrow widths (no horizontal page scroll).
- Unit tests cover the phase mapping (each row of the table) and the board view model grouping; `go test ./...` (including `-race` for web) passes.
- If templates change Tailwind classes, `internal/web/static/app.css` is regenerated with `scripts/build-css.sh` and committed.
- CLAUDE.md "Web UI" section documents the phase sub-columns and the derivation rule.
